The new nullable fields `allowPartialSearchResults` and `allowPartialSequenceResults` are always serialized (writing `null` when unset), while other nullable fields in the same method (e.g., `tiebreakerField`, `waitForCompletionTimeout`, `keepAlive`) are conditionally written only when non-null. For consistency with the surrounding style, consider guarding with a null check:
```java
if (allowPartialSearchResults != null) {
    builder.field(KEY_ALLOW_PARTIAL_SEARCH_RESULTS, allowPartialSearchResults);
}
if (allowPartialSequenceResults != null) {
    builder.field(KEY_ALLOW_PARTIAL_SEQUENCE_RESULTS, allowPartialSequenceResults);
}
```

The first line `payload.shardFailures();` is a redundant call whose return value is discarded. It appears to be a leftover. Remove it:
```java
public static Results fromPayload(Payload payload) {
    List<?> values = payload.values();
    return new Results(
        new TotalHits(values.size(), Relation.EQUAL_TO),
        payload.timeTook(),
        false,
        values,
        payload.resultType(),
        payload.shardFailures()
    );
}
```

The request body fields are written as JSON strings instead of JSON booleans. `builder.field("allow_partial_search_results", String.valueOf(allowPartialSearchResults))` calls the `field(String, String)` overload, producing `"allow_partial_search_results": "true"` (a string). The server-side parser uses `declareBoolean` → `XContentParser::booleanValue`, which expects a JSON boolean. This will cause a parse error at runtime. Use the `Boolean` overload instead:
```java
builder.field("allow_partial_search_results", allowPartialSearchResults);
builder.field("allow_partial_sequence_results", allowPartialSequenceResults);
```
The same applies to the `else if (randomBoolean())` branch where `builder.field("allow_partial_search_results", randomBoolean())` is correct (uses the `boolean` overload), but the `String.valueOf(...)` calls in the `if` branch are not.

Missing `return` after the early-exit `doPayload(listener)` call. After `doPayload` invokes `listener.onResponse(...)`, execution falls through to the rest of `tumbleWindow`, which will call `checkMissingEvents` and eventually invoke the listener a second time (either via another `doPayload` or via `advance`). `ActionListener` must be completed exactly once. Add `return;` immediately after `doPayload(listener);`:
```java
if (allowPartialSequenceResults == false && shardFailures.isEmpty() == false) {
    doPayload(listener);
    return;  // <-- missing
}
```

